1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,811
One prompt.

2
00:00:01,488 --> 00:00:02,571
Five hidden jobs.

3
00:00:03,236 --> 00:00:04,569
And an answer you can't check.

4
00:00:05,183 --> 00:00:07,650
That's what happens when you ask Claude to

5
00:00:07,650 --> 00:00:10,234
analyse a quarter of market data and propose

6
00:00:10,234 --> 00:00:11,761
strategies, all in one go.

7
00:00:12,361 --> 00:00:13,365
Here's where we're heading:

8
00:00:14,028 --> 00:00:16,690
the same request as five small steps, each one

9
00:00:16,690 --> 00:00:17,269
checkable.

10
00:00:17,849 --> 00:00:20,310
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able to

11
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:22,883
break any big business request into a chain of

12
00:00:22,883 --> 00:00:25,456
steps that Claude gets right, and that you can

13
00:00:25,456 --> 00:00:25,848
verify.

14
00:00:26,447 --> 00:00:27,183
Chapter one.

15
00:00:27,921 --> 00:00:29,222
Spot the hidden jobs.

16
00:00:29,823 --> 00:00:31,625
Here's a prompt many of us have typed:

17
00:00:32,246 --> 00:00:35,266
analyse our Q3 market data and propose five

18
00:00:35,266 --> 00:00:36,038
strategies.

19
00:00:36,622 --> 00:00:38,949
It looks like one request, but it hides at

20
00:00:38,949 --> 00:00:39,836
least five jobs:

21
00:00:40,418 --> 00:00:42,917
read the data, spot the trends, explain the

22
00:00:42,917 --> 00:00:45,125
causes, generate ideas, and rank them.

23
00:00:45,702 --> 00:00:48,187
Asked in one breath, the model has to juggle

24
00:00:48,187 --> 00:00:49,317
all of them at once.

25
00:00:49,895 --> 00:00:52,234
The usual result is generic strategies,

26
00:00:52,234 --> 00:00:54,872
skipped steps, or numbers that were never in

27
00:00:54,872 --> 00:00:55,412
the data.

28
00:00:56,011 --> 00:00:58,253
Chain of thought means asking the model to

29
00:00:58,253 --> 00:01:00,548
write out its reasoning before it gives the

30
00:01:00,548 --> 00:01:00,922
answer.

31
00:01:01,513 --> 00:01:03,920
Each step is on the page, so later steps can

32
00:01:03,920 --> 00:01:06,272
build on earlier ones, and you can read the

33
00:01:06,272 --> 00:01:08,734
reasoning to see exactly where it went wrong.

34
00:01:09,315 --> 00:01:11,992
Same model, same data, but more of the work

35
00:01:11,992 --> 00:01:14,670
happens out loud, which usually means fewer

36
00:01:14,670 --> 00:01:16,538
errors on multi-step problems.

37
00:01:17,138 --> 00:01:19,371
Task decomposition works one level up.

38
00:01:19,973 --> 00:01:22,426
Instead of one prompt doing everything, you

39
00:01:22,426 --> 00:01:24,537
break the project into small, ordered

40
00:01:24,537 --> 00:01:25,108
sub-tasks.

41
00:01:25,676 --> 00:01:28,304
Each one has a clear input, a single job, and

42
00:01:28,304 --> 00:01:29,180
a clear output.

43
00:01:29,733 --> 00:01:32,254
Chain of thought improves the reasoning inside

44
00:01:32,254 --> 00:01:32,638
a step.

45
00:01:33,261 --> 00:01:35,861
Decomposition improves the structure between

46
00:01:35,861 --> 00:01:36,215
steps.

47
00:01:36,787 --> 00:01:37,735
You'll want both.

48
00:01:38,335 --> 00:01:38,941
Takeaway:

49
00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:42,494
if a request hides several jobs, give each job

50
00:01:42,494 --> 00:01:43,286
its own step.

51
00:01:43,886 --> 00:01:44,661
Chapter two.

52
00:01:45,344 --> 00:01:46,917
Turn one prompt into five.

53
00:01:47,517 --> 00:01:48,674
Here's the before version:

54
00:01:49,330 --> 00:01:52,038
one prompt, the Q3 data pasted in, and a

55
00:01:52,038 --> 00:01:53,934
request for five strategies.

56
00:01:54,529 --> 00:01:55,530
You do get an answer.

57
00:01:56,184 --> 00:01:58,737
But you can't tell which numbers came from the

58
00:01:58,737 --> 00:02:01,291
data, why these five were chosen, or where the

59
00:02:01,291 --> 00:02:02,179
reasoning broke.

60
00:02:02,779 --> 00:02:03,989
Now the after version.

61
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:05,255
Step one:

62
00:02:05,968 --> 00:02:08,243
extract the key metrics, and quote the source

63
00:02:08,243 --> 00:02:08,496
rows.

64
00:02:09,081 --> 00:02:09,765
Step two:

65
00:02:10,477 --> 00:02:11,953
find the three biggest trends.

66
00:02:12,526 --> 00:02:13,231
Step three:

67
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:15,882
explain the likely cause of each trend.

68
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:17,183
Step four:

69
00:02:17,898 --> 00:02:20,681
brainstorm ten strategy options tied to those

70
00:02:20,681 --> 00:02:21,114
causes.

71
00:02:21,679 --> 00:02:22,407
Step five:

72
00:02:23,074 --> 00:02:25,581
score them on impact and effort, and pick the

73
00:02:25,581 --> 00:02:26,138
best five.

74
00:02:26,711 --> 00:02:29,041
Every output becomes the next step's input.

75
00:02:29,641 --> 00:02:30,684
Phrasing matters.

76
00:02:31,314 --> 00:02:33,548
Plain, think step by step, helps a little.

77
00:02:34,170 --> 00:02:35,775
Better is to spell out the steps:

78
00:02:36,413 --> 00:02:38,495
first do this, then that, then the next thing.

79
00:02:39,201 --> 00:02:40,548
Best for review is structure.

80
00:02:41,219 --> 00:02:43,665
Ask Claude to put its reasoning inside

81
00:02:43,665 --> 00:02:46,304
thinking tags and the final result inside

82
00:02:46,304 --> 00:02:47,076
answer tags.

83
00:02:47,636 --> 00:02:49,957
Now you can read the work, and your code or a

84
00:02:49,957 --> 00:02:51,968
colleague can pull out just the answer.

85
00:02:52,568 --> 00:02:55,135
For bigger projects, run each step as its own

86
00:02:55,135 --> 00:02:57,132
prompt and pass the output forward.

87
00:02:57,752 --> 00:02:59,018
That's called prompt chaining.

88
00:02:59,651 --> 00:03:02,329
Between steps you can check the result, fix a

89
00:03:02,329 --> 00:03:04,590
mistake early, or rerun just one step.

90
00:03:05,187 --> 00:03:07,642
If step two found the wrong trends, you fix

91
00:03:07,642 --> 00:03:09,811
step two, not the whole strategy deck.

92
00:03:10,411 --> 00:03:11,017
Takeaway:

93
00:03:11,791 --> 00:03:14,105
make the reasoning visible, and check each

94
00:03:14,105 --> 00:03:16,088
output before the next step uses it.

95
00:03:16,688 --> 00:03:17,499
Chapter three.

96
00:03:18,195 --> 00:03:19,527
Decompose it yourself.

97
00:03:20,127 --> 00:03:21,179
Pause the video now.

98
00:03:21,831 --> 00:03:22,789
Take this task:

99
00:03:23,438 --> 00:03:25,655
plan the launch of a new product next quarter.

100
00:03:26,303 --> 00:03:27,813
Write four to six sub-tasks.

101
00:03:28,414 --> 00:03:30,985
For each one, note its input, its output, and

102
00:03:30,985 --> 00:03:33,099
how you'd check that output is right.

103
00:03:33,686 --> 00:03:35,945
Then write the prompt for step one, using

104
00:03:35,945 --> 00:03:37,653
explicit step-by-step phrasing.

105
00:03:38,268 --> 00:03:40,448
Give yourself five minutes, then press play.

106
00:03:41,049 --> 00:03:42,543
Here's one possible answer.

107
00:03:43,210 --> 00:03:45,076
Define the audience and the launch goal.

108
00:03:45,707 --> 00:03:46,854
Scan the competitors.

109
00:03:47,478 --> 00:03:49,565
Write the positioning and the key message.

110
00:03:50,129 --> 00:03:51,892
Choose channels and split the budget.

111
00:03:52,566 --> 00:03:54,641
Then build the timeline and list the risks.

112
00:03:55,202 --> 00:03:57,232
Yours may look different, and that's fine.

113
00:03:57,831 --> 00:03:58,437
Takeaway:

114
00:03:59,194 --> 00:04:01,991
every step gets one job, and an output you can

115
00:04:01,991 --> 00:04:02,356
check.

116
00:04:02,956 --> 00:04:03,747
Chapter four.

117
00:04:04,473 --> 00:04:05,646
Know when to use it.

118
00:04:06,246 --> 00:04:08,949
Decompose when the task has several stages,

119
00:04:08,949 --> 00:04:11,274
involves numbers, drives an important

120
00:04:11,274 --> 00:04:14,039
decision, or when someone needs to audit the

121
00:04:14,039 --> 00:04:14,668
reasoning.

122
00:04:15,272 --> 00:04:17,956
Keep it to one prompt for simple lookups and

123
00:04:17,956 --> 00:04:20,519
quick rewrites, where extra steps only add

124
00:04:20,519 --> 00:04:21,373
time and cost.

125
00:04:21,973 --> 00:04:22,579
Takeaway:

126
00:04:23,365 --> 00:04:25,959
decompose when the work has stages, numbers,

127
00:04:25,959 --> 00:04:26,549
or stakes.

128
00:04:27,153 --> 00:04:28,569
Otherwise, keep it simple.

129
00:04:29,169 --> 00:04:29,993
Let's recap.

130
00:04:30,669 --> 00:04:32,264
Big prompts hide many jobs.

131
00:04:32,865 --> 00:04:35,301
Chain of thought and explicit phrasing make

132
00:04:35,301 --> 00:04:36,321
the steps visible.

133
00:04:36,921 --> 00:04:39,728
Each step gets one job, a clear input, and an

134
00:04:39,728 --> 00:04:41,038
output you can check.

135
00:04:41,638 --> 00:04:43,732
And you decompose when the work is

136
00:04:43,732 --> 00:04:46,011
multi-stage, numeric, or high-stakes.
